            <div xml:id="d931">

                <pb facs="0864" n="856" sameAs="frus:frus1940v04/pg856"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.102 Tientsin/683: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Tientsin</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Fletcher</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tientsin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-06-13T12:00:00+09:00">June 13,
                        1940—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received 2:07 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>92. My 80, May 24, 5 p.m.<anchor n="31" xml:id="d931-f1"/> Barrier situation
                    remains unchanged. Indiscriminate search of personal effects of Americans
                    continues undiminished and my written representations to the Japanese Consulate
                    General on May 10 and May 29 requesting that arrangements be made without delay
                    to provide for the unrestricted passage of all Americans and their baggage
                    through the barriers have not been answered or even acknowledged.</p>
                <p>The acting Japanese Consul General yesterday informed me that instructions had
                    been received from the Foreign Office, based on Ambassador Grew’s
                    representations, to eliminate such inspection, that the Consulate General was
                    doing all it could in the matter, but that the authority of “civilians” to
                    effect the change was definitely limited.</p>
                <p>The acting Japanese Consul General also expressed confidence [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">hope?</hi>] that we have a little more patience.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Chungking, Peiping, text by air mail to Tokyo.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Fletcher</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
